text:
Each version is one rendered site, kept in its own directory. The latest render is also copied to /. Older directories stay as they were.
/                         current release, 0.4.0
/v0.3.0/                  the 0.3.0 site, kept as it was
/askr-versions.json       the only version list
/askr-versions.json:
{
  "mode": "inline",
  "versions": [
    {"label": "0.4.0", "root": "/", "latest": true},
    {"label": "0.3.0", "root": "/v0.3.0/"}
  ]
}
inline prints the version on a short title, as in Askr  0.3. title is for a long name: the header shows the name and a chevron, and the version number appears only in the menu. This gallery’s long-name specimen is Versioning Docs with a long title.
Publishing a new release:

Render the tag you are freezing.
Put that _site in /v0.4/.
Copy the same files to /.
Add 0.4 to /askr-versions.json.

Leave /v0.3.0/ untouched when a newer release is published. Its pages, Page List, and Areas stay the text of that tag. In this gallery, 0.3.0 still has Install. It does not have Usage.
The menu keeps the current path. From /usage/versions.html, choosing 0.3.0 opens /v0.3.0/usage/versions.html. That page does not exist in 0.3.0.
Askr does not invent that missing page. The project adds a 404.qmd. When the path contains a version directory, the page sends the reader to that version’s home. This still works when the site is published under a repository prefix such as /askr/:
&lt;script&gt;
var path = location.pathname;
var version = path.match(/(\/v[^/]+)\//);
if (version) location.replace(path.slice(0, version.index) + version[1] + "/");
else location.replace("/");
&lt;/script&gt;
This gallery’s menu is 0.4.0 and 0.3.0. Version 0.3.0 is only its home. Open this Versioning Docs page, then choose 0.3.0: that page is not in 0.3.0, so you land on the 0.3.0 home. longTitle in this gallery’s JSON is only for the long-name specimen above. A project omits it.
